[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The first implementation of this did the redirect in-process and produced
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m spurious mismatches: under `cargo test`'s default parallel harness, libtest
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writes its own progress lines (`test foo ... ok`) straight to fd 1 from other
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m threads, and those landed inside the capture. The capture now happens in a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **forked child** (`common::capture_forked_i32`), which gets its own copy of the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m file-descriptor table, so the redirect is invisible to every other test thread
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the buffer contains only what the library printed. The child returns the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `int` through a separate temp file, so the value and the bytes always come from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the same invocation.
